In article <1993Jun7.015714.25598@Princeton.EDU> acoggins@shade.Princeton.EDU (Adam Cody Coggins) writes:
>IRC is a tool which can be used for business use (e.g. remote conferences),

Business use?  is this documented as ever actually happening? I don't
know about *you*, but I'd be very leery of transacting business over
something that leaks security-wise as much as IRC does.  Channel crashers,
anybody? 

>academic use (e.g. discussion of academic subjects or projects), recreational 
>or any number of other uses (file transfer, combinations of other uses, etc.) 

File transfer?  I thought that's what FTP is for.  Unless you're 
referring to the old hack of getting people to type something that
the IRC client would beam you their /etc/passwd so you could hack it..

>To subject a student to disciplinary measures for using IRC, in absence of 
>prior warnings or policy statements *specifically mentioning IRC* is a case
>of of arbitrary punishment without due process or attempt at fairness.
>It would reflect very poorly upon an institution.

Of course, let's be quite fair about it.  Commercial users have
shied away from using EDI over the Internet due to security concerns.
And I'm sure more sites have gotten broken into due to screwups
in the configuration of ftp/tftp than the IRC hack ever did.

So.. let's see.. extending your logic..

"subjecting a user to disciplinary measures for using e-mail, etc.."
"subjecting a user to disciplinary measures for using FTP, etc.."
"subjecting a user to disciplinary measures for using the C compiler etc.."

So.. Explain to me what's wrong with a policy that says:

"Thou shalt not screw with another user's files, lest thine gonads
be lopped off....", and *NOT* explicitly identify the *means*.

Remember - if you try to enumerate the "offensive" commands, you better
make sure you have a better idea of *exactly* what's on your system
than all the systems that got bit by the Morris worm attacking
sendmail and fingerd.  Are you confident that you know *all* the
holes like that and are willing to enumerate them? ;)

Policies should enumerate prohibited *behaviors*.  In general, the
number of offensive behaviors is a *lot* shorter than the number of
commands that can be used to *implement* the behaviors.  I'd have to
check, but I think Virginia Tech's policy statement fits on 1 page and
only lists 8-10 categories of problem behavior.  I'd be lucky to fit
the *names* of the commands on a large Unix system into 5 pages.

