valdis@black-ice.cc.vt.edu (Valdis Kletnieks) writes:

>>IRC is a tool which can be used for business use (e.g. remote conferences),

While it is true that IRC 'can' be used for such things, I am not
aware of this actually happening.  If someone can cite a recent
example, I would like to hear about it.  It is true that companies and
organizations can (and have) set up private IRC networks totally
disconnected from the 'main' one, but this is a different matter
completely.

>>academic use (e.g. discussion of academic subjects or projects), 

This is much more common.  Channels like #UNIX, #LINUX, and so on are
there to share information on specific topics.  Granted, too often
such discussion is 

>>recreational or any number of other uses (file transfer,
>>combinations of other uses, etc.) 

>File transfer?  I thought that's what FTP is for.  

FTP is not the only means of file transfer on the net, nor is it the
One True Method, or even the most efficient when two people simply
want to trade files.  IRC has facilities (DCC) to send any form of
file from one person to another it's much quicker than setting up a
temporary FSP server, and much 'safer' than finding open directories
on FTP sites.

To be fair, IRC has quite a few legitimate uses.  The idea of forbidding
its use simply because it is considered recreational or trivial does
not hold water.  The problem with IRC is not the client program using
CPU time, or the existence of IRC channels that could (*gasp*) be
used for recreation.

FLAME MODE ON:

The problem that most people who actually use IRC have stems from the
total lack of intelligence and continuity in IRC administration.  At
most sites, the requirements for becoming an IRC server operator are
1) be intelligent to compile something and 2) find another server
operator willing to provide links.

This has lead to incredible anarchy on the network.  Rogue IRC server
operators, *most* of whom consider the net a playground and themselves
to be part of some kind of elite group of net.gods.

