>Indeed, but it is my experience as a system manager that while you can mount an
>NFS filesystem, you cannot change the file access of remote files, so those files
>which don't have read access to root you can't read. Maybe I was doing the wrong
>thing: I didn't try very hard, as I was just trying to read something in my
>(civilian ;) filespace.

Not relavent.

If you can mount a filesystem remote, and you are root, you can "su" to any
user ID you wish, which immediately grants you access to that user's files
on the remote machine -- possibly excepting root (depending on how the
filesystem is exported).

Note that "user ids" are <numeric> in reality and have no inherent tie to
the textual string you usually type in :-)

Thus, exporting a filesystem is equivalent to allowing access to the entire
tree of user-owned files if the mounting client has root privileges.

