ENDURING THE MLA CONVENTION

Joseph S. Salemi

The annual gathering of the Modern Language
Association is always an affliction. One signs up and
prepares for the ordeal with the same apprehension
that one brings to an appointment with an oral
surgeon. Partly this has to do with the convention's
sheer size--over the years it has ballooned into a
thing of grossly inhuman dimensions. Thousands of
participants, registrants, and job seekers jostle each
other for four straight days in three major New York
hotels. In that time there are over five hundred
separate sessions where scholarly papers are
presented and discussed, running almost non-stop
from 8:30 in the morning until 10:15 at night.
Dozens of social events and business meetings dot
the schedule, while entire floors are given over to
publishers' exhibits. The atmosphere in the halls,
lobbies, and conference rooms resembles that of a
busy airport--crowds of anxious people are headed in
many different directions, clutching floor maps and
timetables with an indefinable sense of urgency.
And, of course, there is the secret MLA convention of
several hundred job interviews--those nervous
encounters behind the closed doors of hotel suites,
where the careers of young aspirants to the academy
are made or cut short.

In fact, it is impossible to say that one has "attended"
an MLA convention. One might as well have tried to
attend D-Day at Normandy. The operation is too big
and too complicated to allow an individual registrant
anything more than a partial view of what is going
on. One can be present--as I was--at about twenty
sessions, but even that exhausting number is only a
fragment that the limits of time, energy, and interest
allowed me. Nevertheless, I saw and heard enough at
last December's gathering of the Modern Language
Association to present our readers with a substantial
sampling of what passes for scholarship and
intellectual discourse in the American academy
today.

I used the words _fragment_ and _sampling_, but it
would be more accurate to say _biopsy_slide_.
Nothing that the MLA's toughest conservative critics
have written about its follies over the last decade
prepares one for the metastasis that has overtaken
what used to be the premier scholarly organization
for literary studies in this country. Those ideals
which an association of humanists should cherish--
academic freedom, scholarly independence,
politically uncompromised research, merit-based
recognition, unfettered debate--are now as empty
and meaningless to the reigning ethos of the MLA as
was the Roman republic under the Caesars, or the
German Reichstag under the Nazis. There is one
major difference. In Nazi Germany and imperial
Rome rhetorical homage--owever insincere--was paid
to the empty shells of the old order. But the new MLA
scorns such hypocrisy. The prevailing spirit of the
MLA, as expressed in its 1992 convention, is explicit
rejection and contempt for all these earlier ideals
except insofar as they can be given--in good
Orwellian fashion--a meaning consonant with the
changed conditions of academic life.

The MLA--though richer and more powerful than it
has ever been--is now a scholarly organization in
name only. It is dominated not just by politically
correct lemmings (they are endemic to teaching) but
by partisan activists of a very rancorous and
combative disposition who have turned the
association into a vehicle for the politicization of
pedagogy in this country as part of a radical social
agenda. Academic freedom, in MLA-speak, now
means the freedom of left-liberals, feminists,
Marxists, minorities, and gays to be hired and
tenured without the inconvenience of competition or
the necessity of producing real scholarly work.
Unfettered debate means arguing about various
nuances in current theoretical trends, but never
subjecting those trends to any serious criticism, and
no debate is so unfettered as to escape the scrutiny
of campus sensitivity codes. Scholarly independence
now goes under the preferred names of
professionalism and collegiality, which taken
together seem to mean a slavish adherence to the
guild regulations of your least imaginative
colleagues. Merit-based recognition means
recognition of the merit inherent in belonging to a
certified victim category. As for politically
uncompromised research--well, you would be hard
put to find anyone in the MLA who believes that
politically uncompromised research is possible. It is
now received dogma among _bien-pensant_
academics that all scholarly activity--the gathering of
data, criticism, commentary, annotation, and even
the editing of texts--is totally and inescapably
political. In a usage shift that I find ominous, MLA
members no longer refer to themselves as a
community of scholars united in a love of learning,
but as _the_profession_. The term is redolent of
conformism and groupthink, and is almost always
uttered in the same tone of voice that Catholics once
used when speaking of Holy Mother Church, or that
Communists employed when referring to The Party.

In fact the study of the humanities (one can sense it
in the muted hush of reverence that descends
whenever academics allude to "the profession") has
become a kind of secular religion, replete with
doctrinal orthodoxy, ritualized deportment, and even
mannerisms of speech. The MLA has public prayer
and sermons (in the form of general addresses to the
assembled convention); a sacred language (in the
form of poststructuralist jargon); hagiography (in the
form of adulation given to trendy scholars and avant
garde writers); canonical books (in the form of
politically correct works that are being
sledgehammered into the curriculum); heresy-
hunting (in the form of attacks on individual
scholars and literary works that refuse to serve a
left-liberal agenda); and above all the same sense of
exclusionary solidarity that one might feel at a
gathering of some self-sequestered splinter of a
fundamentalist sect. The profession--such as it is--is
now firmly in the hands of proselytizers who see
literary studies as a front for sociopolitical
missionary work, carried on under the guise of
esoteric scholarship, protected by job tenure, and
liberally assisted with government grant money.

The best place to start analyzing what has happened
to the MLA is the session entitled "Celebrating
Twenty-Five Years of Radical Caucus." Presided over
by Paul Lauter of Trinity College (Connecticut), this
was a gathering of aging Marxists, curriculum
reformers, feminists, gay activists, and other
_engage*_ types who represent the most radicalized
segment of the MLA's membership. The "Radical
Caucus"--of which Lauter is the secretary--is one of
the major engines of politicization in the MLA, and
has been so for a quarter of a century. Lauter beamed
as he addressed this crowd, who had assembled to
congratulate themselves on their spectacular success
in taking over so much of the machinery and the
ethos of the MLA. He pointed out how back in the
late sixties several antiwar activists (himself
included) decided to apply to the stodgy and
conservative MLA the same radical tactics that had
been used by agitators to oppose the French war in
Algeria, and the Vietnam war. Through
demonstrations, disruptions, political pressure, and
ultimately infiltration, any organization could be
subverted and turned to radical purposes.
Accordingly, a "Tactics Committee" was formed in
1968 in "an effort to direct radical energy at MLA," on
which Lauter, Richard Poirier, and Grace Paley were
active. The fruits of this early effort are apparent in
the MLA of 1992, now as blatantly partisan for left-
liberal causes as a Democratic clubhouse. It is
interesting to note that this scenario faithfully
replicates the standard tactical directives found in
revolutionary manuals. First one insists to an
unsuspecting public that all one's partisan agitation
is spontaneous and activated by grassroot forces.
Once in power, however, the revolution's leaders can
safely take credit for the careful and purposeful
planning of the successful assault.

More interesting than the history of this takeover,
however, was the larger picture of radical
interconnectedness that emerged from Lauter's
account. This is a fact about which the academic left
is somewhat ticklish, except when talking privately.
Lauter pointed out that the effort to harness the MLA
into a politically activist role was absolutely
inseparable from those less overtly political
questions of curriculum, the canon, gender, and
pedagogical innovation that the Radical Caucus
raised at the same time. In other words, these now
trendy subjects were specific items on a radical
political agenda for American higher education as
early as the 1960s, and their prominence in literary
discourse today is not a function of their intrinsic
value, but due to the fact that a coterie of agitators
has pushed them to the fore. Lauter boasted that the
Radical Caucus promoted the first Marxist literary
group at MLA, that it was instrumental in founding
the magazine Radical Teacher soon after,  and that it
was among the first to raise the issues of patriarchy,
feminism, and racism in the context of literary
studies. In short, the face of the profession today is
the face that the Radical Caucus deliberately gave to
it starting around 1968. One should keep this in
mind the next time an earnest liberal colleague
argues that the latest absurdities and trivialities in
contemporary criticism are merely natural
developments of an ongoing humanistic tradition.

Present also at this session was an aging Grace Paley,
now the Mother Jones rediviva of the academic left.
For her presentation, Paley read a selection from one
of her stories in progress--a tedious screed about the
supposed economic oppression of Puerto Rico by the
United States. Her radical audience ate it up, and here
one saw in a nutshell the effect that politicization
has had not only on scholarship in the humanities
but also on literature itself. Paley may be a fine
writer, but the undertow of political activism has
swept both literature and literary studies into a sea
of partisan rancor, controversy, advocacy, and sheer
querulousness. Her story was typical of the texts
that the MLA now wants to read, teach, and comment
on: fictionalized political tracts, slogans dressed up
in metaphor, stylized social protest, and calls for
mobilization. As I listened to the cheers that
followed Paley's presentation, it occurred to me that
this sort of thing had by now attained the level of a
genre: first the passionate indictment of a wrong,
then the rhetorical appeal to left-liberal reflexes,
followed by the catharsis and solidarity of applause.
Indeed, this mini-drama had become so routinized at
the MLA as to constitute more of a fashion statement
than an expression of conviction, and nearly every
session had an instance of it.

This is not to say that the dominant factions in the
MLA are completely at ease and free from
apprehension. The flap over political correctness
two years ago rattled them badly, and there were still
some signs of defensiveness on the subject. For
example, the MLA Women's Caucus--another radical
subgroup--arranged two sessions that ostensibly
dealt with some of the fallout from that controversy.
Both sessions were entitled "Answering the Enemy,"
the first tackling the issue of how tenure and
promotion are granted in academia today, and the
second addressing the question of what is occurring
in the classroom. This general title was both
amusing and revealing--the "enemy" is apparently
anyone who dares to question the current radical
hegemony on college campuses. The choice of such a
word to characterize one's critics betrays on the one
hand the siege mentality of a threatened in-group,
and on the other a recognition that disputes over the
direction of academia are now at the point of
combat. Predictably, neither of these sessions took
notice of (much less answered) a single substantive
complaint raised in the numerous critiques of higher
education that have appeared over the last decade.

Incidentally, one might have thought that by now the
expression "politically correct" had endured enough
polemical crossfire to guarantee that it would be
employed gingerly, if at all, by the academic left. But
this has not happened. There were three sessions
entitled "Can Popular Culture Be Politically Correct?"
Lest you think that a trace of irony hung about these
titles, I assure you that at the MLA the phrase
"politically correct" is still used, in deadpan
seriousness, as an epithet of honor. These sessions
all concerned themselves with forms of popular
culture (film, TV, rock music, and advertising), and
to what degree they conformed to left-liberal criteria
of political virtue. Not one panelist questioned the
merit of politically correct conformism; they all
simply praised or blamed popular culture insofar as
its manifestations could be measured on a political
yardstick. Incidentally, the new MLA consciously
directs as much attention (under the vapid rubric of
"cultural studies") to things like advertising and
popular music as it does to literary texts. This is
another example of the multitude of channels
employed by academic revolutionaries to push the
world that exists beyond the academy in a politically
desirable direction.

The MLA has also come to function, quite
inadvertently, as a kind of museum for intellectual
artifacts. You don't usually find many committed
Trotskyites, Sartrean existentialists, or champions of
Esperanto these days, but they do show up at this
convention. The political death of Marxism, for
instance, seems to have had no effect whatsoever on
the prevalence of Marxist role-playing among
privileged academics. But it is a pale, gone-to-seed
sort of Marxism, manifesting itself mostly in a
certain attitudinizing about class and "capitalist
structures" without the ideological bite of the past.
At one session I attended, a middle-aged professor
from New England went on about his working-class
roots, his "solidarity" with the working class, and his
heroic efforts to get his composition students to
swallow a "class analysis" of their social status. It
seemed hardly worthwhile to remind him that, as a
full professor with a salary in the high five figures,
he was hardly "working-class" in any meaningful
sense. But then this was typical of the sort of self-
deception that permeated the MLA Convention:
people with tenured sinecures teaching 3-2
schedules (with frequent sabbaticals) luxuriated in
the fantasy that they were daring, bold
revolutionaries who at great risk to themselves were
struggling to help oppressed minorities and the
proletariat.

Another very troubling symptom of the academy's
malaise was the unadulterated venom and bile that
seeped out of the discourse at every turn during this
conference. A number of colleagues and
acquaintances in various fields noticed and
mentioned it to me. Disagreements were never
friendly, but always personal and vindictive. Terms
like "conservative," "fascist," "reactionary,"
"traditionalist," "sexist," and the ubiquitous "white
male" were heard on every side, in tones ranging
from pitying contempt to sheer fury. Political
correctness had nurtured a palpable animus among
radicalized faculty that made the entire convention
seem like a page out of _The_Screwtape_Letters_. For
example, I attended a session entitled "Race, Class
and Gender in Scholarly Editing," which was one of
several panels dedicated to this holy trinity. In the
course of discussion a number of vicious references
were made to "conservatives in the academy,"
including Carol Iannone and the late Alan Bloom. In
particular, Professor Brenda Silver (Dartmouth)
alluded contemptuously to Jeffrey Hart as "a
conservative columnist." I checked my program, and
confirmed that Silver was indeed a member of the
Dartmouth faculty. In other words, although Jeffrey
Hart is a full professor and former chair of the
English department at her own school, Silver could
not bring herself to acknowledge him publicly as a
colleague. Since he was the enemy, he could only be
called "a conservative columnist." Try to imagine the
poisonous atmosphere of a school such as the one
where Silver teaches, and you have some idea of
what a politicized academy means in human terms.

An illustrative incident occurred when the
irrepressible Paul Lauter gave a talk at a session
entitled "PC, Multiculturalism, and Literary Value."
Working himself up into a veritable paroxysm of bile,
Lauter called the conservative critics of
multiculturalism and the new theoretical trends in
scholarship "a rearguard fungus, rapidly
deliquescing." I'm not sure what to make of that
sally-- a rearguard is part of an army, and fungi do
not deliquesce. But perhaps Lauter's venom made
him mix his metaphors. In any case, the very same
day I attended another session that touched on the
subject of "hate speech" and the necessity of
squelching it whenever it appeared in one's
classroom. Professor Lisa Jadwin (St. John Fisher
College) insisted that any general negative
characterization of a group constituted "hate
speech," and had to be answered or rebuked
severely. I told Professor Jadwin what Paul Lauter
had said that morning about academic conservatives.
Was it hate speech? "Yes, it's hate speech," Jadwin
answered without hesitation. "Fine," I replied. "I'm
going to quote both of you in print."

As a matter of fact, I disagree with Jadwin. I think
Lauter has every right to excoriate his enemies
verbally in whatever degree he sees fit. It's just that
he's not a particularly effective polemicist, as his
"rearguard fungus" blunder demonstrates. He
showed some promise when he attacked Arthur
Schlesinger's new book on the disuniting of America
--Lauter referred to it as "that infernal little book of
his." This was marginally better, but hardly world-
class polemics.

I have one real piece of news to report. At a session
dealing with political correctness, Catharine R.
Stimpson (Rutgers), the primadonna of Catharine PC
operatics, actually admitted that political
correctness was "a problem in many of our schools."
While accusing the academy's conservative critics of
using "hyperbolic and manipulative" language,
Stimpson grudgingly admitted that "some PC is
there," and that left-liberals had shown "an
insufficient skepticism about identity politics."
Stimpson also pointed out that on some campuses
political correctness had become "prissy,
demanding, self-righteous, and intolerant of
disagreement," and she allowed that "many
intellectual and political errors" had been made by
the backers of multiculturalism. But Stimpson's
overall attitude was one of supercilious smugness--
according to her, all is well in academia despite a
minor problem or two. The fact that Stimpson felt
compelled to make any concessions at all on this
subject is a sign of how utterly out of control
political correctness is in higher education today.

How different was the very tough-minded and gutsy
talk delivered by Professor Leonard Ashley (Brooklyn
College) called "Watch Your Mouth: 'Political
Correctness' and Restraint on United States Speech
Today." With considerable courage, Ashley tore into
the "dangerous mind control, the radical pettiness,
and the rampant malice" of current academic
discourse, and he pointed out how rather than
expanding the possibilities for debate, the new
speech codes and imposed verbal etiquette were
limiting thought in America.

When asked by a hostile listener for an example,
Ashley told his audience (in what had to be the finest
moment of the convention) something that they
probably did not expect to hear. "Professor Richard
Levin was gang-banged in PMLA," said Ashley, "for
daring to question Marxist and feminist assumptions
in his own field of specialization. They went after
him, and tried to silence him. How's that for an
example?" The audience, despite their anger, kept
their mouths shut after that.

Incidentally, this convention might well have been
called the Feminist MLA, considering the sheer
volume of feminist sessions and papers. But it was a
feminism still unsure of itself, defensive, and
skewered by its ideological compulsions. I attended
one session entitled "The Institutionalization of
Feminist Literary Criticism," where several ladies
discussed their efforts to put together a dictionary
of feminist theory. What struck me as funny was the
epistemological bind that their radicalism,
relativism, and identity politics had gotten them
into. How could they assign items to be written
without considering the race, class, and sexual
orientation of the women assigned the task? Would
there be enough minority and gay women to write an
acceptable percentage of the entries? Should
contributors identify their racial, social, and sexual
background as a preliminary to their submissions?
And since as a group they eschewed "fixed bodies of
knowledge," how could they put together something
as atavistic as a dictionary? None of these questions
were resolved, despite a good deal of debate.
However, it was clear that the dictionary was going
forward, since generous grand money was backing
the project.

Towards the end of this session someone asked if
the feminist literary scholar Camille Paglia would be
included in the dictionary. There was consternation,
and a few red faces. "No," said a panelist. "She should
be forgotten!" The image of the memory-hole in
Orwell's _1984_ came to my mind. There were laughs
and jeers from the mostly female audience, and one
person volunteered this brittle remark: "Put her in a
subcategory. List her under has-beens!" This was
said by a graduate student with no publications at a
time (late 1992) when Paglia's newest book was
being distributed and reviewed. Extrapolate from the
malice and pig-ignorance of that feminist graduate
student and you have a picture of the American
academy today.

Rarely in life is there perfect closure--art normally
provides us with the aesthetic satisfaction of a
symphony's grand finale, an English sonnet's closing
couplet, or a good novel's denouement. Yet by the
sheerest serendipity the 1992 MLA convention came
to an amazingly appropriate finish. One of the very
last sessions had the irresistible title "Should
Literary Criticism Contribute to Politics?"--a question
which, in the context of the entire MLA experience,
had to be either a joke or a provocation. The session
also promised heavy-duty fireworks, for the two
major speakers would be Edward Said (Columbia),
one of the more notorious purveyors of academic
trendiness; and Richard Levin (SUNY-Stony Brook),
the scholar who had been mentioned by Professor
Ashley as the victim of a savage attack by radical
feminists in an earlier issue of PMLA. The subject of
the panel was to be the relation of poetics, politics,
and literary criticism, and everyone expected a very
pointed exchange of views. As a result, attendance at
the session chamber was so packed that the hotel
administration was compelled, for safety reasons, to
move it to the grand ballroom.

Levin presented a straightforward defense of the
literary text as an "autonomous unified work, shaped
by an author to provoke aesthetic pleasure." He
granted that this autonomy and unity could never be
complete, and that external forces had some bearing
on a reader's response, but nevertheless literary
works did show cohesion and intelligibility which
could be traced to the intentions and efforts of their
creators. Furthermore, said Levin, taking pleasure in
a literary text was a perfectly legitimate part of the
critical act, and our "political interests do no
necessarily coincide with our aesthetic experiences."

Such remarks, in the politicized atmosphere of the
MLA, were like a red flag to a bull. There was angry
buzzing and a few half-suppressed jeers all over the
ballroom. But Levin persisted, and argued that
among all the possible options for literary
scholarship, the option to consider a work of
literature _qua_ literature--that is, in itself and on its
own intrinsic merits as a work of art--was just as
valid and legitimate as any other approach. He ended
with a courageous comment about some of the
advocates of politicizing trends in current literary
criticism. They don't want their approach to be "one
of many," said Levin. They want their approach to be
the only legitimate one, and that is why they hate
and resent pluralism in the academy.

Parts of the audience were now seething, and there
probably would have been some bitter exchanges
had it not been Said's turn to speak. And here came
the biggest surprise of all. Though Said had some
harsh things to say about the neoconservative attack
on the academic left, and although he defended
"engagement and commitment" as essential to
scholarly work, he insisted that politics should be
kept out of the classroom. Said declared that it was
"irresponsible and cowardly" to use one's classroom
for political activism, and that he himself had never
intruded into his lectures the political controversies
in which he is involved. Said went on to say that,
while literary study might provide us with the
rhetorical tools that we need to make a difference in
the world of power-relations, we should "always
address inequities in the public sphere, not in the
classroom." And he ended by warning the MLA that
"the American academy provides us with luxurious
academic freedom." If we abuse it, argued Said, by
trying to establish politically correct orthodoxies in
place of old ones, then this freedom would very
likely disappear.

No one knew what to say. A presumed chief guru of
the politicized academy had just committed public
treason before his assembled peers. The audience
was at a loss, and there was a good deal of
murmuring and private discussion until one enraged
male graduate student jumped up to shout: "The
university is the real world! It's implicated in the
corporate structure of this rotten capitalist society!
You are up on that panel, and teaching at Columbia,
because you live off the slave labor of part-time
faculty!" This outburst set off an explosion of
shouts, cheers, and applause, and the graduate
student who had made it stormed out of the
ballroom before Said had a chance to answer him.

As it happened, however, neither Said nor Levin had
much to do with the real blaze that was ignited at
this session. A third speaker on the panel, Diana
Fuss (Princeton), began her talk by declaring that she
had decided to alter the listed title of her paper to
"Fellatio," and that she was dedicating it "to the
muscular security guards here at the Hilton." As she
gestured towards the several uniformed men
present, Fuss said "This one's for you, guys." There
was a scattering of laughter in the audience, but also
a troubled murmur.

Fuss went on to deliver an utterly absurd discourse
on orifices, oral eroticism, and castration anxiety
that had no discernible structure or logical sequence
other than a sustained hostility to Sigmund Freud. As
I understand her argument, Freud had "rhetorically
legitimated political inequalities" with his comments
on perversion and his myth of the mouth-anus
polarity. The paper was bizarre in the extreme, but
not a surprising performance in the world of
looniness that constitutes literary study today.

At the end of the session, an enraged woman got up
to address Professor Fuss. She screamed "I object to
your vulgar dedication of your talk to the hotel
security guards! They should not be the butt of
jokes! This is classism! You are publicly humiliating
working-class people in a setting where they cannot
respond!" Someone else jumped up and added "These
men are victims of your insensitivity--their
discourse is not heard here at the MLA!" A vast
clamor of applause and cheers arose from the
audience in the ballroom. Fuss had to return to the
podium and abjectly apologize--to her questioners,
to the audience in general, and to the security
guards.

Who was it, I thought, who said that the Revolution
eats its own children? Political correctness is indeed
a capricious taskmaster. Sexual liberation and the
breaking of bourgeois conventions apparently do not
allow for vulgar remarks about fellatio, especially if
such remarks are directed towards "the working
class." Fuss had attempted to be funny, but her joke
had fallen afoul of the ideological rage that fuels the
modern academy.

It also occurred to me that the scene I had just
witnessed would have been unimaginable in the
prerevolutionary MLA. No university professor in
1963 would have dreamt of making tasteless and
vulgar insinuations in the course of presenting a
paper, and no scholarly audience would have roared
approval or disapproval like drunken louts at a
hockey game. These earlier teachers were not
"repressed" or "out of touch with their sexuality" or
any other such nonsense--they simply would have
known, without even considering the matter, that
this sort of behavior was beneath them. They would,
as Richard Levin put it, have discussed literature
_qua_ literature. Whatever their failings and
limitations, those past scholars enjoyed two things
that are conspicuously absent from the MLA, and
indeed from the entire American academy: the ability
to do scholarly work with a measure of detachment
and good will; and the freedom to say what they
wished without being shouted down by the self-
appointed spokesmen for certified victim groups.

A conference as dominated by intellectual mutants
as the MLA deserves to be called "the academic Gong
Show," as John Leo has rightly christened it. The
convention provides outsiders with plenty of
occasions for spoofing and ridicule. But this is the
real tragedy of the collapse of American higher
education in the humanities. Behind the hilarity that
imbecilic session titles and political posturing evoke
is the unspoken assumption of American pragmatism
that anything said or done in classrooms by English
teachers is essentially unimportant. We can tolerate
the MLA, says the disinterested observer, because it
is so utterly uninfluential. But is it? The indifference
of the public to the florid pathological symptoms so
visible at the MLA Convention means that intellectual
discourse in this country will remain firmly in the
hands of ideological multiculturalists, radical
feminists, Queer Theorists, diehard Marxists,
nihilists, and an entire spectrum of impostors and
incendiaries who have entrenched themselves in our
universities. These people will determine the
vocabulary of debate--and indeed the very
parameters of acceptable thought--for Americans
who go to college. If you think that such a situation
will have no long-term consequences, remember how
Paul Lauter and his minions in the Radical Caucus
took over and transformed the MLA itself.

To be sure, there is still some genuine scholarship
alive at the MLA. It survives furtively and
unobtrusively, in sparsely attended sessions where
hardworking individuals labor with real love and
zeal to understand a particular author, a special
work, or a literary mode. Every so often I found
myself at a panel where a truly luminous intelligence
was visible, elucidating a text with grace, wit, and
insight. How rare was the experience, and how alien
to the general tenor of the new organization! I
consoled myself with the thought that as long as
men and women could be enchanted by what we
once called _belles_lettres_, there would always be a
few such scholars--the saving remnant, as it were, of
the world of learning. But I also reflected that these
individuals would survive in the American academy--
if they survived at all--only by accident. Most
colleges and universities did not want them, and
they certainly no longer had a home in the Modern
Language Association.

