From caf-talk Caf Jun  8 15:25:30 1993
From: jmc@SAIL.Stanford.EDU (John McCarthy)
Subject:  Measure 116: sexual harassment
Message-ID: <JMC.93Jun8083726@SAIL.Stanford.EDU>
Date: 08 Jun 1993 15:37:26 GMT

SEXUAL HARASSMENT: SEPARATING THE GOATS
FROM THE SHEEP

E. L. Pattullo

It is long past time to talk sense about sexual
harassment in our colleges and universities. Clearly,
the feeblest imagination can grasp the plight of a
young woman, student or junior faculty member,
faced with demands for sexual favors from a
professor who promises to help or destroy her
career. Teachers who thus abuse their power are
guilty of grave malfeasance and should be drummed
out of the profession. It is similarly easy for women
(if sometimes less so for men) to empathize with
females simply hassled by peers who will not take
no for an answer or by clods who subject them to
lewd remarks, unfunny sexist jokes, or fanny-
pinching of one kind or another. The range of
actions that may offend is enormous. Law and
common decency demand that the colleges take
steps to curb such behavior. However, common
sense demands that those steps reflect the
distinction between boorishness and blackmail.

In law and at most colleges the definition of sexual
harassment begins with some variation of "unwanted
sexual behavior." The modifier immediately
introduces an element of subjectivity that means
identical acts may or may not constitute an offense.
The problem is further complicated by the great
variety of behaviors potentially actionable. Finally,
the offense is usually committed in private and
leaves no tell-tale marks. These circumstances, plus
recognition of the nuance inherent in sexual
interchange, should prompt caution in all who seek
justice through third-party intervention. Right or
wrong, absent a confession or witness, a scintilla of
doubt about who did what to whom exists whenever
harassment is charged; as, for instance, in the Anita
Hill/Clarence Thomas imbroglio. Victims themselves
are often ambivalent, frequently coupling their
complaint with the plea, "I don't want anything bad
to happen to him." Sometimes, no doubt, this is
prompted by the complainant's knowledge of her
own complicity, now greatly regretted. Occasionally,
I suspect, it reflects understanding that the intimacy
essential to the best teaching can cause an unwary
teacher to overstep the bounds. Often, it simply
exemplifies the misplaced generosity of youth.

Former Harvard Dean Henry Rosovsky stumbled over
the intricacies of the problem in 1984 when he wrote
to all faculty members, advising them that "amorous
relations" between teachers and students for whom
they have "professional responsibility" are "always
wrong." He quickly heard from Professor J. K.
Galbraith, as follows:

"Just over forty-five years ago, already a
well-fledged member of the Harvard
faculty on a three year appointment, I fell
in love with a young female student... A
not wholly unpredictable consequence of
this lapse from faculty and professional
decorum, as now required, was that we
were married. So, and happily, we have
remained... As a senior member of this
community, I am acutely conscious of
my need to be an example for younger
and possibly more ardent members of
the faculty. I must do everything
possible to retrieve my error. My wife,
needless to say, shares my concern. What
would you advise?"

The Dean, recalling the Church's ancient practice of
selling indulgences, suggested that endowment of a
chair might be a suitable penance!

Most ardent feminists resent any levity in the
treatment of so serious a subject. But progress in
resolving the problem will quicken if we all lighten
up a bit and separate the relatively few serious
offenses from the mild harassment that is a
lingering, regrettable hangover from the era before
the sexual revolution, a time not far past.* Education,
not prosecution, is the way to address the latter
problem. Peccadilloes (which may, of course, be
infuriating) prompt many complaints that can
usually be resolved through a sophisticated advising
system that gives victims--commonly young,
troubled and uncertain--easy access to effective help.
Often a complainant's need is satisfied by providing
nothing more than moral support, enabling her to
handle the situation herself. In other cases the
simple fact that the victim seeks assistance is
enough to bring the offender to his senses. Those
remedies failing, a decanal warning (written or oral)
will usually suffice. Harvard, with an admirable
grievance network, reports that over a five year
period, 95% of the complaints registered were
satisfactorily resolved in these ways. Most
complainants were quickly extricated from an
awkward situation and enabled to get on with their
lives with a minimum of uproar. Many if not all of
the offenders learned, without undue cost to
themselves or the community, that the day is past
when they can get away with the peccadillo, let alone
more serious harassment.

But then there are those serious cases, usually
involving a teacher who consciously uses his
authority to try to extort sexual favors from a
student or subordinate. Such conduct is
unforgivable. Unfortunately the quiet discretion
which appropriately accompanies the handling of
peccadilloes is all too often extended by most
institutions also to their handling of these offenses.
The community is seldom told about the offense or
the offender or the discipline inflicted, making it
unlikely that punishment, however severe, will act as
a deterrent to others. In addition, silence creates a
Kafka-esque situation in which the rumor mills grind
fine and the notion that complainants are themselves
mysteriously tarnished is perpetuated. Because the
latter delusion persists, those who have fallen prey
to a demonstrable gross malefaction should be
encouraged (though never "outed" or even pressured)
to go public.

Secrecy is defended in two ways. First, as necessary
to convince victims that reporting the harassment
will not result in unwelcome publicity. Second, on
the ground that only a promise of confidentiality will
persuade culprits to accept punishment without
going to court. Neither argument is necessarily
persuasive. Surely, the identity of _victims_ can be
shielded, even while offenders are punished, as is
common in cases of rape. In any case, the victim
should have a choice. The second excuse probably
has some validity. However, if--as one earnestly
hopes--stringent discipline is invoked only when
evidence of malfeasance is highly persuasive, it
seems unlikely that many offenders will wish to fight
the verdict in public. (In fact, I'm told, few true
harassers plead total innocence; it is the extenuating
circumstance that most conjure.)

I suspect abhorrence of adverse publicity is the
principal factor prompting institutions to deny the
public information about the existence, let alone the
details, of serious cases of transgression. That is
easy to understand--and to regret. Amongst other
things, one can't help but wonder how guarantees of
confidentiality jibe with the institution's
responsibility for protecting future victims and
other colleges from what is, after all, a sex offender--
a category of crime notorious for recidivism. What
prevents the culprit from moving elsewhere, with an
apparently clean slate, free to continue his behavior?

In summary, sexual harassment can be of many
levels. If comprising elements of blackmail it can be
an absolute nightmare, making life miserable for the
victim and seriously threatening her future. Sexual
harassment, also, can be simply an intolerable
nuisance that's hard to stop without outside help.
Finally, what one person sees as harassment another
may welcome as a titillating bit of sexual by-play;
many a young woman has enjoyed reciprocal
flirtation with an admired teacher, sometimes going
beyond bounds her mother would approve. Given
these truths, every institution must create a network
of sympathetic, easily accessible advisers, who give
complainants prompt and dependable support while
distinguishing indiscretions from gross violations of
trust and seeking remedies appropriate to each.

In acting to combat harassment, colleges must guard
against letting themselves get bent out of shape.
Universities, above all, should be jealous guardians
of free speech, insisting that First Amendment
protection extends even to vulgarians whose crude
antics and dirty talk disturb the climate of civility we
also highly prize--and find so difficult to maintain in
this turbulent era. Deans and presidents should
beware of stumbling into attempts to police the
manners and morals of students; their parental role
has outlived its acceptability. Amongst obstacles to
be avoided is that posed by the zealots of feminism--
young and old, straight and lesbian--who are eager to
use the existence of a problem, sometimes serious
but often trivial, as a club with which to pound the
opposite sex and further an agenda only partially
shared by the majority of either gender. Most people
know that men and women are not different species
and that hairy-handed males are not the only sex that
enjoys sexual games in their infinite variety.
Increasingly few women are shrinking violets,
entirely unable to rebuff unwelcome behavior on the
part of peers, mentors, or teachers. Many men remain
oafs, but men (and boys) are learning. They will learn
faster if they see that authorities do not confuse
protection from sexual harassment with license to
impose upon the academic community a latter-day
Puritanism.

Ours is a complex culture with mores differing
somewhat from class to class and place to place. But,
there is no serious disagreement about an absolute
prohibition on teachers using their authority to force
sexual favors from students or staff. It's only in the
area of flirtation that disagreement--and plain
misunderstanding--sometimes exists. We must learn
not to make a Federal case out of the tendency of
healthy men and women to make passes at others,
with or without glasses. Equally, we can't leave those
who are easily victimized alone and defenseless and
at the mercy of professorial predators. The answer is
to throw the book at gross offenders, letting the
world know about it, while providing, if needed,
quiet and effective assistance to those  unhappily
entangled in the often ambiguous charades that mark
our human sexuality.

________________________________

E. L. Pattullo is a retired Director of the Center for
Behavioral Sciences, Harvard University.
* It is worth noting that this "revolution" has two
divergent prongs. On the one hand, it encourages
greater sexual involvement/activity which requires
rapprochement; on the other, it legislates noli me
tangere. These two tendencies have not been
reconciled yet.

5



