Actually, you are incorrect also--but you're getting close.

When it comes to *constitutional* limits, governmental funding is not the
key ingredient.  (If it were, then all private universities receiving
governmental funding--which is virtually all of them in the US--would also
be subject to the restrictions.)

Constitutional restrictions affect institutions that act as agent of
the state (the proverbial "arm of the state").  Funding usually has little
bearing on an institution's status as an agent of the state.  The key
generally rests on whether the government maintains institutional
control.

In other words, a "public university" is "public" in terms of constitutional
issues because the government *controls* the institution NOT because it
*funds* the institution.  (Institutional control can be, for example,
maintaining legislative control over policy-making, having governmental
selection of the trustees, etc.; however, having politicians hold ex
officio positions as trustees has specifically been ruled not to constitute
institutional control.)

As such, A public university does not have the option of giving up
governmental funding in exchange for being able to violate the constitution.
A public university has no choice but change its behavior, if it is found
to be violating the constitution.

What you may have been confusing this with are a number of federal *statutes*
that make funding contingent upon meeting certain restrictions.  (For
example, the Civil Rights Act of 1964, or FERPA.)


