did anything even requiring sanction at all?

Anyhow, to follow your reasoning and by example, suppose you got two
speeding tickets in one year. You wouldn't expect to be summarily
executed on the spot.

You might well expect a little harsher treatment for the second
ticket, if the judge noticed, but ultimately you would expect the the
punishment to fall somewhere within what is expected for the
particular crime, a harsh fine perhaps? $100? $200? How much can we
possibly expect for merely risking the life and limb of motorists on
the road and willfully breaking one of the most fundamental traffic
laws?

Even suspension of license for just two tickets, even if the two
tickets were received in the same day, would generally be considered
overly harsh unless there was something else going on (I suppose we
could always hypothesize more and more circumstances.)

Now, going back to this particular case, the student printed out the
"/etc/passwd" file, and was suspended for a year etc.

The fundamental questions are not only is this a bit harsh for the
"crime" in question, something of somewhat less risk to life and limb
than exceeding the speed limit for which most of us expect, even for a
second offense, no more than perhaps $100 or so in fines and that's
that (pay the clerk), but whether this is rationally can be taken as a
