We will always have rules with "fuzzy edges" in that rules always
require interpretation.  So, don't worry about that part of it;
you are sure to get your wish.

That does *not* mean that we shoud *set out* to draft vague rules.
Vagueness is, variously, an invitation to selective enforcement (as
perhaps in the case of "KC") and an invitation to user misuse on
the grounds that the rules could be interpreted so as not to prohibit
the intended behavior (as perhaps in the case of "KC").

I think you are right: Rules do not (should not) replace humans.  We
ought to investigate the particulars surrounding an incident.  What
should be done depends on what happened.

I think it is a grave error, though, to hold that the proper process
may only proceed in an environment without explicit rules or with
intentionally ambiguous or otherwise vague rules.  If mischevous or
adventurous users want to push rules to the edge--and some do--or
authoritarian or responsible sysadmins wnat to enforce the letter
of the law--and some do--the rules need edges and the law needs
letters.  (Smilies for the adjectives modifying "users" and
"sysadmins" above, please.)  The alternatives are disasters bound
to happen.

IMHO.

