------------------Appeal letter-
                                                  May 24, 1993

Dr. William F. Munson
Dean of Students
University of Houston

Dean William F. Munson,

     This letter is an appeal to the sanction given to me by
Asst. Dean of Students, Kathy Anzivino on May 17, 1993 concerning
the alleged violations that I have committed relating to the use
of computer resources.
     I feel that an immediate reopening of the case should be
reinstated for the following reasons which I will highlight in
the subsequent sections.
 
     1. Vagueness of Charges.
     
     The notification letter from the Dean of Student's Office
(4/29/93) required that "[I] contact [Kathy Anzivino's] office
within three days after receiving this letter to discuss possible
violations of the University's disciplinary code." 
     Federal courts have found that such notices fail to give any
idea of the offence which had been committed or what rule had
been violated1. In addition, courts have held that students have
a right to know the charges sufficiently in advance of the
hearing to permit them "to examine the charges and prepare a
defense and gather evidence and witnesses."2 In fact the courts
have found the lack of specificity of the charges adversely
affects the student's ability to prepare his defense and thus the
meaningfullness of his opportunity to be heard.
     Hence it becomes clear that the original notification letter
prevented my ability to provide an adequate defense, since it
fails to give any specific policy with I have allegedly violated.

     2. Wording of Charges Changed During Conference.

     Upon arriving at the conference (4/29/93), I learned that
the official charge against me was that I had illegally accessed
password information by printing out the "/etc/passwd" file. 
Indeed, in the memo from Bill Schubert to Kathy Anzivino dated
4/28/93, Mr. Schubert stated that the printout is of the form:

          USERID :*: encrypted password : description

     I pointed out several times to Ms. Anzivino that the
"/etc/passwd" file does not contain encrypted passwords or any
information leading to user passwords.  The actual format of each
line in the "/etc/passwd" file is as follows:

          USERID :*: uid : gid : description : home dir. : shell

where the asterisk takes the place of where each user's encrypted
password _used_ to be, which are now stored on a hidden system
level file called "master.passwd."

     Thus, the "/etc/passwd" file is not a file of passwords,
rather it is a user list file.  Ms. Anzivino called Mr. Schubert
on the phone (during the conference) to confirm this, and after
consulting with his co-workers, Mr. Schubert agreed.  Note that
Mr. Schubert never appeared during the conference, which
prevented me from addressing him directly, again obstructing my
ability to adequately defend myself.  Then, after disconnecting
with Mr. Schubert, Ms. Anzivino decided to change the specific
charge.  She now claimed that merely accessing the "/etc/passwd"
file constitutes unauthorized use of the computer system. 
Specifically, Ms. Anzivino had changed the charge, during the
conference, from "accessing password information" to "using the
computer account for purposes other than what it is to be used
for."  
     However, using a computer account for the purpose of
accessing the "/etc/passwd" file can not be construed as
"unauthorized use or misuse of the computer system" for the
following reasons:

          (A) The "/etc/passwd" file can be accessed by the
          'finger' command, which lists various information on
          users on MENUDO.  For example, the command

               finger @@menudo.uh.edu

          yields the same information as found in the file
          "/etc/passwd."  This command can be called from
          anywhere on the network, even by those not on UH
          systems.

          (B) The MENUDO Unix (Ultrix system) manual defines the
          "/etc/passwd" file as "User information," and implies
          that it is a publicly accessible list of users.

          (C) In fact, access to the "/etc/passwd" file is
          required every time a user logs in, and every time a
          user does a directory listing of any files on the
          system (including his own).

     In summary, I formally request a reopening of my case and
for it to appear before the University Hearing Board.  I feel
there was an innocent misunderstanding during the conference
between Ms. Anzivino and me.  I also believe she has
misinterpreted the facts presented by Support Services, and thus
I ask you if it is possible to remedy this situation.

                                   Sincerely,


                                   James Henderson 
