LIBERALS AREN'T BOTHERED BY MERE FACTS
Jeffrey Hart

     When the church bells rang out in Warsaw, Prague, Budapest
and Leningrad--now restored to St.Petersburg--we had good cause
to rejoice.  The communist phase of human history was coming to
an end in Europe.  Beijing and Havana would no doubt soon fall to
the spirit of freedom.
     But wait.  The impulse to remake human nature has not left
us.  That impulse was powerfully recorded in Plato's Republic but
doubtless existed much earlier.  And today the egalitarian
impulse thrives despite the death of communism.
     Perhaps today it exists in its purest and most poisonous
form on the university campus, but it is also a potent force in
general culture.
     Radical egalitarianism asserts, to put it in general terms,
that it is impossible to judge that one thing is better than
another.  Thus we have "multiculturalism," which ostensibly
asserts that one culture is as valuable as another, but really
has it in mind to assert that various primitive and Third World
cultures are actually superior to the advanced Western nations.
If Herodotus thought that the culture of Athens was superior to
that of Xerxes' Persia, well, he needed consciousness raising.
     Freshmen arrive on my Ivy League campus awash in this
drivel.  They have been brainwashed in secondary schools, both
public and private.
     Their cultural egalitarianism usually collapses after a few
sharp questions.  What do they think of the former Hindu custom
of suttee, in which the wife threw herself, or was thrown, on her
husband's funeral pyre?  What do they think of the African
practice of clitorectomy?  What about the female foot-binding
practiced in Mandarin cultures, or the tens of thousands of human
sacrifices conducted by the Aztecs or the infant sacrifices
carried out by the Carthaginians?  It is not widely understood
that the tribes surrounding the Aztecs lived in such fear that
they welcomed the Spanish conquistadors.
     The Spanish often behaved badly.  But today we judge them
not by Aztec standards but by European and Christian standards--
as, indeed, many writers did at the time.
     Radical egalitarianism also asserts that one mode of sexual
behavior is as good as another, throwing overboard the
accumulated wisdom of millenniums about such matters.
     The new secretary of the U.S. Department of Health and Human
Services asserts that homosexuality is just another lifestyle,
and in that she is certainly in tune with university culture.
     Yet from the beginning of human history every culture we
know of has frowned on homosexual practice.
     The egalitarian passion has invaded the arts as well.  Thus
the president of the Modern Language Association, which has
30,000 professors as members, denies that there are differences
of quality in literature.  Professor Houston baker of the
University of Pennsylvania considers such claims elitist.  He
says that a preference for Virginia Woolf over Pearl Buck is like
a preference for a pizza over a hoagie.
     In the current mode of university English departments, the
label on a ketchup bottle and an ode by Keats are equally
"texts."
     Radical egalitarianism has seriously upset the relations
between men and women, casting aside previous assumptions about
decorum and role.  Thus it is reactionary to assert that most
women are not designed to be marine Corps platoon leaders.
     From its policies on homosexuals in the military to its
desire to penalize economic success, the Clinton administration
is the most radically egalitarian within memory.  Somehow the
administration believes that it has the moral right to raise
taxes drastically on people who invested wisely or luckily or
built a better widget.  The Clinton White House apparently thinks
that it knows what "fairness" is in terms of economic reward.  It
is operating on the pure egalitarian imperative.
     Does Clinton not know that the vast majority of people did
better during the wicked 1980s?  And that people in the upper
brackets paid more taxes than previously--because they profited
from the boom?  Mere facts have nothing to do with the
egalitarian poison.

