CONSERVATIVES ARE WINNING IN IOWA STATE'S PC BATTLES

     Iowa State University has been the sight of political
controversy for the past few months and many students say the
controversy isn't over yet.  Issues keeping the campus preses hot
included:  membership in the United States Student Association
(USSA), an alleged physical assault by one student senator on
another senator, a school funded assistant for a homosexual
student group and a contested student election.
     The political divisions among students  began in March when
a delegation of students was chosen to attend the USSA conference
in Washington, D.C.  The controversy erupted over the amount of
time allowed for students to apply to attend the conference.
Iowa State representatives of USSA released the application forms
only two days before the deadline for completion.  The trip was
organized through the Government of the Student Body (GSB), Iowa
State's student government.  The application process was
criticized by the GSB's Finance Director Heather Hobson.  In an
interview with The Daily, Iowa State's official paper, Hobson
said, "I think it's too short of a time for the application to be
returned."
     GSB senator John Stineman echoed Hobson when he told The
Daily, that the selection process was unfair because it didn't
represent "all walks of life."
     In a hurried manner, a delegation of 23 students was chosen
to attend the four day conference.  Several of the students costs
were not paid by GSB, including Mike McNarney's, a representative
from The Daily.  The students whose travel was paid for by Iowa
State's GSB, which is funded by mandatory student fees, racked up
over $6,500 in costs.
     ISU's delegation was the largest by far to attend the
conference.  On average, ISU had at least four people at every
workshop held.
     In D.C. the USSA controversy grew as allegations over a
physical assault were leveled by one delegate to another.  GSB
senator Margaret-Ann Cavett charged fellow GSB senator Randy
