     As campaigning for the government offices increased,
students vying for ISU's top positions were squaring off on the
issue of mandatory student fees being used for political
purposes.
     Presidential candidates Denis Klein  and Pete Rimsans
clearly  voiced their opposition to funding USSA. Taking an
opposite position on the issue was candidate Robert Easterling.
     Easterling's running mate, vice-presidential candidate Brian
Lemberger, told The Daily that USSA is a "bargain" and said its
political agenda will "keep students from getting screwed over."
