SOCIALIST PROFS STILL TRYING TO START REVOLUTION
Robb Jennings &
David W. Almasi

     While the majority of Americans celebrated the Easter
weekend at home with their families, the annual Socialist
Scholars Conference (SSC) was in full swing in the heart of New
York City bringing some 500 Marxists together to assess the
progress of their movement.  This year, unlike conferences before
it, many of the scholars began to openly question the people and
the policies that have guided the movement since its heyday in
the 1960's.
     In his opening letter to conference attendees, SSC organizer
and CUNY Grad Center Professor Bogdan Denitch wrote:  "We need to
address specific problems with our proposals and to organize
broad coalitions to push a timid, centrist [Clinton]
administration to address long overdue problems: decent jobs;
shorter work weeks, work years, and work lifetimes; equality; an
end to racism and gender discrimination; available decent
housing; a single-payer national health policy; repeal of anti-
labor legislation; outlawing scabbing; support for human rights
at home and abroad....  However, all these reforms, no matter how
urgent and desirable, only serve to humanize a basically
exploitive capitalist system which must be transcended if a truly
human world community is to ever emerge....  We need a
fundamental transformation, in a word, a democratic socialism."
     SSC organizers were again unable to ensure racial and gender
equality in the panels.  In previous years, this has led to
animosity to the extent that Queer Nation "zapped" the 1990
convention by storming the stage of the opening session (to the
applause of the scholars).  To quell discontent, organizers
promised last year panels without minority or sexual diversity
would not be allowed.
     An "explanation" in the program, however, indicated the
scholars could not live up to their promise.  The apology read:
"Our Committee has repeatedly stressed, cajoled, and pleaded with
organizations and individuals submitting panels that we want
these to be sexually and racially as balanced as it is humanly
possible to make them....  This year we have refused to schedule
any panels which were either all male or racially homogeneous in
those slots we control....  We hope this gently pushes this
year's sinners into mending their ways for next year.  At least
it should make them take this issue seriously."
     Their threat, however, was not backed up by much muscle.
Organizers actually controlled only two theaters, and their
policy extended only to the plenaries and a mere 24 panels out of
a planned 143.  Other panels could pick and choose their racial
and gender make-up as they pleased.  Many did, breaking the
taboo.
     While the panelists at the conference may not have met a
diversity quota, certainly the topics discussed were widely
varied.  Panel discussion topics ranged from "Rank and File
Revolts in the New York Area:  Postal service 1970 to Present" to
"Sadomasochism, Science and Power."  While many topics discussed
seemed to ignore the economic reality of socialism, the panelists
were always able to relate the discussion to the plight of the
"workers".
     "The Democratic Left and Hemispheric Solidarity" was the
title of the conference's opening session.  Speakers included:
Denitch, Princeton's Cornell West, Harvard's Elaine Barnard and
Congressman Major Owens (D-NY).  The text of each speaker's
presentation varied, but overall opposition to the North American
Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA) was woven throughout the evening.
     "It should be called 'Shafta' not NAFTA," Barnard told the
400 plus member audience. "We know, of course, there is no such
thing as free trade. It is an ideologically bloated term.  NAFTA
re-regulates trade in a very adversarial way for most workers,"
she continued.  Barnard, a Canadian citizen and a former New
Democratic Party (NDP) senator in Canada, seemed a bit
hypocritical in her defense of workers.  National Review recently
reported that a Canadian labor union withdrew its membership from
NDP because of a disagreement over NDP's support for affirmative
action policies, which the union claimed would actually hurt
workers.
     Owen's speech was unique because he was not only the most
prominent elected official at the conference but he was also
openly critical of his own party's leader.  Owens attacked
President Bill Clinton's Democratic Leadership Council calling it
a conservative think tank, and he condemned President Bill
Clinton for being a conservative.
     Owens concluded his speech and declared his political
leanings by saying, "I hope we can create a possibility of
solidarity being forged by that hardcore nucleus in this country.
I hope that socialists, between conferences, will find their way
to do something significant."
     The most popular and most radical speaker, at the opening
session, was Princeton professor and Democratic Socialists of
America vice-president Cornell West.  In a fire and brimstone
manner, West railed against the American establishment when he
said,  "It is so hypocritical to see how our nation/state can
spend all the money necessary to contain rage and fury but rarely
come up with the money to deal with the quiet riots that are at
work this very moment."
     On NAFTA, West was clear about his opposition, "We're
talking about new strategies for capital.  The deregulation, the
unleashing of a new wave of market forces, and the link of the
marketization of the world and the gangsterization of the world."
     With the stage set at the opening session, the conference
panelists attacked the mainstream economic and cultural base of
America.
     "I've been asking myself a lot since this whole process of
organizing this group began, uh, what does it mean to be Italian-
American when I'm also a socialist of working class background
and a gay man as well,"  George DeStefano said of his work with
Italian Americans for a Multicultural U.S.(IAMUS).  DeStefano was
one of the panelists discussing "Being 'Ethnic' and Being
'White'", a forum designed to show similarities between ethnic
whites and traditional minorities.
     The panel's goal seemed admirable, but after much discussion
it became evident IAMUS' purpose was to organize Italian
Americans to oppose the celebration of Christopher Columbus and
the "Rudy Giulliani's" of the world.  Giulliani is a former U.S.
Attorney and former Republican nominee for mayor of New York.
     In "The Politics of Culture," Manning Marable of the
University of Colorado at Boulder complained that minorities are
doing the "master's work" by failing to differentiate between
ethnicity and culture  "The existing system," he said.  "That's
the problem."  He cited black-oriented magazines like Ebony, Jet,
and Black Enterprise as forcing black integration into white-
dominated culture by focusing on black success stories within the
white realm.
     This fear of current black success was echoed by Frank
Kirkland of Hunter College, who praised the message conveyed by
the radical rap of Ice Cube, but bemoaned that the rapper still
works within the white economy.
     Taking the more radical tact was West when he said he feared
the struggle against the "perennial everyday incessant white
supremacist bombardment against black humanity, black
intelligence, black beauty, black capacity, and black
possibility" fosters "black invisibility."  Black culture, he
said, must be used to fight this bombardment.
     West, who has appeared on "Donahue" with Sister Souljah,
noted black cultural contributions have been more powerful than
political ones, said:  "If we had politics that was one-tenth as
rich as our music, the White House wouldn't be white."  He noted,
however, that the left has not been effective in capitalizing on
this culture, pointing out that black youth tend to think of
themselves as outlaws, and act accordingly, under the influence
of radical rap.
     Speaking on black conservatives, Marable compared former
leftists-turned-conservatives like Thomas Sowell and Stanley
Crouch to the RCA/Victor corporate logo--the dog listening to
"his master's voice."
     Marable, a dead ringer for abolitionist Frederick Douglass,
let his free market proclivities show when it was announced that
he was moving to New York.  It was revealed he was considering
teaching at the prestigious--and private--Columbia University.
When the moderator, a CUNY student, tried to sell Marable on the
CUNY system, Marable (as well as panelists and audience members)
laughed in amusement.  Marable, because of his stature as a black
scholar, can demand a high salary and other perks.  He and the
other panelists knew this bargaining chip would be squandered in
the CUNY system, despite the fact that it's faculty boasts of it
as the home of the real left.
     Multiculturalism isn't enough for Jose Alfaro, a teacher at
the "alternative" Central Park East School.  During the panel on
"Multiculturalism and Anti-Racist Education," he said that he
would like to extend the curriculum to make it inherently anti-
racist, anti-sexist, anti-homophobic, and pro-justice.  The basic
goal of the curriculum is not just to learn history, "but also to
actually take part in changing that history."  Besides more
parent involvement in the school, Alfaro also felt giving
students the run of the school was another path to empowerment.

