College Profs Not As Liberal

     A new study by two Ohio State University professors reports
that U.S. college professors have become somewhat more
conservative since 1969.
     The study, which appears in the journal Social Forces and
was reported in USA Today, finds that the number of professors
calling themselves liberal declined from 40.6% in 1969 to 33.8%
in 1984.  Teachers classifying themselves as moderately or
strongly conservative increased from 27.5% to 33.8%.  Finally,
26.6% called themselves moderate in 1984 compared with 27.1% in
1969.
     The findings are based on data from Carnegie Foundation
surveys in 1969, 1975, and 1984.  The professors say no data is
available for the past nine years but that they believe there's
been little change in professor's attitudes since 1984.


Minority Students Complain of Insensitivity

     Minority students at William Penn College in Iowa threatened
to leave the school after complaining that the school did not do
enough to recognize Black History Month.
     Then at two forums on campus, students protested the lack of
minority faculty members and accused the administration of being
insensitive to their views.  Julie Maxwell, president of Students
for Black Interest, said that their only choice might be to
leave.  In response, administrators set up committees to deal
with the students' concerns.
     Meanwhile, at the University of Oregon, students said that
the departure of several minority faculty members were making
minority students feel uncomfortable.
     "Students feel like: Now, we're here and we're going to be
left by ourselves,'" said Leslie Warren, president of the Black
Student Union.
     A coalition of university groups called the Students of
Color Building Bridges presented a petition to administrators
asking the university to increase the number of minority faculty
members and also threatened to leave if their demands were not
met.
     The university has hired five new minority faculty and is
negotiating contracts with several others under the school's
"target-of-opportunity program."

Sister Souljah Declares War

     "Raptivist" Sister Souljah emphatically declared, "We are at
war," in a speech entitled "A Push for Unity" which she delivered
at the University of Houston last month.
     The gist of her arguments was that blacks should rid
themselves of the fetters that were forced on them by racism and
brain-washing.  She suggested they unite in an army to liberate
Africans throughout the world.
     Stressing that racism is supported by a system of power,
Souljah remarked, "You know a state of war exists any time
anybody tries to take from you... your ability to think, to
control your own thoughts and ideas."
     Souljah has been quoted as saying: "If black people kill
black people every day, why not have a week and kill white
people?  In other words, white people, this government and that
mayor were well aware of the fact that black people were dying
every day in Los Angeles under gang violence.  So if you're a
gang member and you would normally be killing somebody, why not
kill a white person?"  She also, however, claims that she is not
a racist and that nobody of African descent can be a racist.
     She also has stated: "I want what's good for my people
first.  And if my survival means your destruction, then so be
it," and "if there are any good white people, I haven't met
them."
     "The first thing that you should know in the African
tradition is that I means we.  What that means is that I am you,
and you are me, and we are us, and us is them, and them is they,
and we are us, and we are all in this together," stated Souljah,
who stressed unity and collective responsibility.
     Souljah has recently spoken at Harvard University, the
University of Florida, Cornell University and the University of
Northern Colorado.  At the latter campus, she was given $6,500
for her speech.

Fish Faces Opposition

     Twelve professors at Indiana University signed a letter
opposing the rumored appointment of Stanley Fish to a top
administrative position there.
     Noting Fish's record of favoring political correctness over
free speech and questioning his commitment to academic freedom,
the professors wrote that Fish's "administrative record and
expressed opinions on the subject of academic freedom should be
sufficient to disqualify him from serious consideration."
     The group cited Fish's statement in 1990 that members of the
National Association of Scholars should not be allowed to serve
on certain committees at Duke University, where he currently
teaches.  The NAS is a national group of professors which favors
traditional education over politically correct multicultural
education and which defends Western culture against multicultural
attacks.
     Fish is said to be a finalist for the position of vice
chancellor for academic affairs and dean of the faculties at
Indiana's Bloomington campus.  Neither he nor IU officials would
confirm this, though.

Students Arrested in Berkeley Take-over

     Forty-six students were arrested and four others sprayed
with mace when they and a hundred others clashed with police
after occupying an administration building at the University of
California's Berkeley campus.
     The students were demanding that the Native-American, Asian-
American and Chicano studies programs which currently make up the
Department of Ethnic Studies each be elevated to department
status in a new "Third World College," according to the Chronicle
of Higher Education.  The student protesters hoped that the
elevated status would attract more money and more students to the
programs and that they would have more control over their
budgets.
     The arrested students were cited with trespassing but then
released.  Administrators say they are considering reorganizing
the ethnic studies department but have made no definite plan

