TA'S RUNNING A HIGH PRICED DIPLOMA MILL
Martin Anderson

     A few days ago, Yale University announced that the four year
cost for tuition, room and board would be $100,440.
     The same week, a report was released on a poll taken of ivy
League college students last fall.  That survey--the most
comprehensive one ever done--consisted of 3,119 face-to-face
interviews with undergraduates at Harvard, Yale, Princeton,
Dartmouth, Cornell, Columbia, Brown and Pennsylvania.  The poll
results were somewhere between embarrassing and shameful.
     Twenty-three percent of these students did not know how many
justices were on the Supreme Court.  Seventy-five percent could
not pick Abraham Lincoln's name from a list of six people as the
man who used the phrase: "government of the people, by the
people, for the people."
     One third did not read a daily newspaper.  Thirty-five
percent did not know that Alan Greenspan is the chairman of the
Federal Reserve Board.  Half of them did not know the names of
the U.S. senators from their home states.
     We expect these young men and women to be the best and the
brightest of their generation, the purported leaders of this
nation a few decades from now.  Instead we are treated to a
spectacle of seemingly ignorant oafs.
     Perhaps we shouldn't be surprised.  Polls of this sort do
not test intelligence, they test knowledge.  And it has been an
open secret for some time that many of our best universities
don't give much of a damn about teaching undergraduates.  As one
of my colleagues at Stanford once remarked, "Professors consider
students the crab grass on the lawn of academe."
     The clearest proof of the disdain that professors at
prestigious schools have toward undergraduates is the extent to
which major teaching responsibilities have been turned over to
other students.  These pseudo-professors are usually called,
misleadingly, "teaching assistants."  They do virtually
everything we think professors do.
     The teaching assistants sometimes lecture to large classes,
they often lead smaller discussion sections where the most
crucial teaching should take place, they make up and grade
examinations, they give final course  grades that become part of
the undergraduates' official academic record--they even counsel
students in both academic and career matters.
     The so-called teaching assistants are usually graduate
students barely a step or two ahead of the students in their
charge; some of them are even undergraduates.  As a group, they
are manifestly unqualified to tackle the enormous responsibility
the university entrusts to them.  Not a one of them could land a
job on the faculty of a comparable university.  Why?  Simply
because they have not earned their degree; they have not yet
demonstrated enough mastery of a subject to be qualified for the
Ph.D.
     The fact that students are teaching and grading
undergraduates is the secret shame of most of our finest
universities.
     At many universities, these fake professors number in the
hundreds.  A recent check showed that Harvard employs about 850
teaching assistants.  Yale has 925.  Princeton has 550.  Columbia
has more than 600.
     The Ivy League is not alone.  At the University of Chicago,
there are more than 330 of these ersatz professors.  On the West
Coast, Stanford has 800, while Berkeley's total is a staggering
1,742.
     The extent of this spurious teaching is so great as to call
into question the legitimacy of the degrees given by those
universities that use students for professors.
     Many Ivy League universities are dangerously close to
becoming $100,000 diploma mills.  Nobody really cares what one
student tells another, and especially no one cares what one
student thinks about another student's work.
     Perhaps what we will have to do is grade university degrees
by the year in which they were granted, much as we now grade fine
wines.
     For example degrees earned in the 1940s and 1950s would be
ranked highly.  Degrees earned during most of the 1960s would be
OK, although the late 1960s might be somewhat suspect.  And many
of the university degrees earned during the 1970s and 1980s may
turn out to be worth not much more than the paper they are
printed on.

