FEMINISTS' WAR ON WORDS GOES SUBSVERSIVE

     A covert battle is raging between the forces of the PC and
advocates of free speech on campus.  The aggressors are feminist
and other such professors and the battleground is, once again,
the vocabulary.  But this battle is different from those which
have raged in the past because it is very much a secretive,
subversive fight.  It is one in which the warriors of the left
covertly change and amend the English language, arming themselves
with new definitions and etymologies for words and phrases, many
of which would seem trivial to mainstream America.  Once they've
completed their lexicographic revisionism, they head back into
their classrooms and lie in wait, anxious for the moment when an
innocent student--or even fellow professor--will use one of their
chosen hot words and, as if he had stepped on a mine, set forth a
barrage of attacks from the femi-warriors.
     Shawn Brown's experience with a feminist teaching assistant
and a left-wing professor at the University of Michigan is case-
in-point (see December, 1992 and April, 1993 Campus Reports).  In
this instance, the innocuous use of the word "stud" in a paper
for a 100-level political science class led to allegations of
sexual harassment and a forced withdrawal from the class by the
shocked and frightened Brown.
     Another example of this type of overreaction comes from
Stetson University in Florida.  A department chairman found
himself under attack for word-misusage after he used the phrase
"rule of thumb" during a faculty meeting.  No sooner had he
uttered the words then a young female professor jumped out of her
chair and berated him for being insensitive and sexist.  She was
appalled that he could use such a phrase.  She claimed amazement
that he didn't know that the term was derived from the rule in
Medieval England that the sticks men used to beat their wives
could not be thicker than their thumbs.
     In fact, few people other than this young woman knew that.
The term "rule of thumb" is generally attributed to a method
brewmeisters used to judge the temperature and consistency of the
beer whereby they stuck their thumb in the vat and decided, from
experience, whether or not it was finished brewing.  This, in
fact, is the etymology attributed by the Oxford dictionary of the
English language.  Nevertheless, the young woman did succeed in
publicly humiliating a distinguished department chair and in
communicating the message that any speech, act, or gesture which
the feminists on campus interpreted to be sexist would not be
tolerated.  She left the department chairman, and other
professors like him, both frightened and dismayed.
     These word warriors of the left now have a new tool in their
battle over verbiage which defenders of the English language
should be aware of.  It is a feminist dictionary called Amazons,
Blue Stockings, and Crones by Cheris Kramarae and Paula A.
Treichler, and it seeks to rewrite and redefine the English
language from a feminist point of view.  Among the new
definitions and etymologies which this book puts forth are the
following:
*    cramps:  "a male dominated religion that keeps women out of
the decision making processes..."
*    exorcism:  "The process of 'peeling off the layers of
mindbindings and cosmetics...movement past the patriarchaly
imposed sense of reality and identity.'"
*    nakedness:  "Means liberation, a joyful and non-neurotic
sexuality for some people; 'for others, it stands for a
licentiousness which threatens traditional moral standards.  Both
of these seemingly contradictory attitudes rest on a common
assumption: that the exposed body is emotionally charged and
potentially subversive.'"
*    bitch:  "the main term used by a member of the (male)
oppressor group to a member of the (female) oppressed group who
challenges the oppressor on his use of oppressive tactics."
*    necrophilia:  "male attraction or need for female energy--
not in the sense of love for actual corpses, but of love for
those victimized into a state of living death."
*    wimmin:  "We have spelt it this way because we are not women
neither are we female.... You may find it trivial--it's just
another part of the deep, very deep rooted sexist attitudes."
     In this last example is the key to the leftists battle plan.
They attack that which mainstream America finds trivial--and
thereby go unnoticed in their work until it is too late.  They
sit in their university offices quietly changing the spellings
and meanings and histories of the thousands of independently
trivial words and phrases which make up the English language and,
for the most part, go unnoticed in their work.  Until the whole
American vocabulary has been changed.  And they, as the authors
and revisionists, are in control.  The defense must arm itself
with the truth--and stop the verb brigade before the whole
language falls.

