You know, we're neglecting an important point in this episode. 

Despite the apparent near-farcical nature of the charges
against KC, the admins did exactly what any "good" policy
would require of them -- instead of administering some
punishment themselves, they forwarded the matter to the
University's due process authority.  

The problem, in this case, is that the judicial authority
(apparently, the Dean of Students' office) did not seem to
possess the technical literacy required to render judgement
on these charges.  Let's face it -- any Unix-literate per-
son should get a hearty chuckle from the notion that a sha-
dowed /etc/passwd is some sort of Secret System File.

We would hope that the bureaucrats would ask someone, "what
the heck is /etc/passwd, anyway?"  If they ask the very
people who filed the charges, they may not get a straight
answer. 

Now, I'll be the first to admit that I don't expect every
bureaucratic administrator to be computer-literate.  On this
campus, that would require knowledge of PRIMOS, VMS, CMS, MVS,
six flavors of Unix, Macintosh, DOS, and (probably) several
other operating system/platform combinations; networking
literacy would require knowledge of Novell, AppleTalk, TCP/IP,
routing/bridging, and more.  How, then, can we solve this problem?

Should we:
	- refer such matters, in a double-blind fashion, to
	  a third-party sysadmin as a "technical consultancy"?

	- convene an ad hoc panel of third-party sysadmins to 
          brief (or make recommendations to) the bureaucrats on 
	  such matters?

	- make such a panel a standing advisory organization, rather than
	  an ad hoc committee?

I would think that such actions would tend to create a more consistent 
approach to policy matters across the disparate organizations of most
universities.  

For instance, most Unix admins on this campus know that I would laugh, 
loud and long, if I were asked to recommend disciplinary action against 
someone who printed /etc/passwd.  Now, the directory entries on a VM/CMS 
system (which contain plaintext passwords) are a different matter; those 
are NOT publicly accessible, and it would take some nefarious work to 
secure a copy of those entries.  So, "snagging the password file" on
a Unix system is QUITE different from doing the same on a VM/CMS system.
Can the bureaucrats enforcing our policies recognize these distinctions?
If not, how should we assist them?

--Wes

