You're close.  In the 'shadow password' scheme, encrypted passwords are
removed from the world readable /etc/passwd file and placed into a file
that only root (ie the passwd program or sysadmins) can read.  It is this
new file containing ONLY the encrypted password that is refered to as the
'shadow passwd file'.  The old passwd file (/etc/passwd) then contains
a pointer to the shadow password file.  Example, here is my entry from 
/etc/passwd:

michaelb:##michaelb:4148:4000:Michael Rogero Brown:/home1/grad/michaelb:/bin/tcsh

The entry '##michaelb' is the pointer to my encrypted password in the shadow
password file.  In the old days, my encrypted password would have been there.

