You clearly do not understand what time, place and manner restrictions are,
in terms of constitutional law--nor do you understand content-based
regulation.  Time, place and manner restrictions are specifically *devoid*
of consideration of content.

A rule prohibiting shouting in a library is a time, place and manner
restriction.  A rule prohibiting shouting the word "Fuck!" in a library
is a content-based regulation.

Check up on the law.  You can start with Clark v. Community for Creative Non-
Violence, 468 U.S. 288 (1984).

If the FCC indecency restrictions were nothing more than time, place and
manner restrictions, then the same restrictions could be presumably placed on
any forum.  The fact of the matter is that the FCC indecency restrictions
are treated by the courts as a distinct area of law--and their restrictions
are *entirely* content-based in nature.

Lest you have some confusion, the fact that a word can be said in some
contexts does not imply that no content-based regulation is going on.
Controlling the context and use *is* controlling the content.

If the government were to say you could use the word "asshole" but just not
in conjunction with the President, do you think that, too, would be a
regulation unrelated to content?


