  I think that this is more of a problem with institutional computing in
general, than just one at academic sites. In many companies, users have
little input on the actions that MIS takes. Also, many MIS departments
have large turnover rates (3-5 years). With conditions like those, I
really don't think that you can have a good administration/user relationship.
  Here at Rice, we've got a student advisory committee and several policy
discussion groups. While students may not be able to change policy, at
least they can discuss it in a public forum with the people implementing
it. Also, many admin. types were former students here, and understand us.
  Sonat had a good MIS department when I was an intern there, for similar
reasons. They have a low turnover rate, which they're very proud of, and
MIS has a steering committee composed of people from other departments. When
I was there (more than a few years ago), one of their goals was to get
the secretaries computers set up the way they wanted. (They wanted to
be able to hotkey between their word processing and mainframe communications
software. They also needed their computers to be print servers for their
bosses machines.)
  If you don't already have a mechanism set up and in use for a dialogue
to take place, expecting one to spring up automatically when something
goes wrong is pretty unrealistic.
