SQUEAKY CHALK

     How To Get It Off Your Chest: Who says nothing interesting
ever happens in New Jersey?   Just last month, at Douglass
College, the women's part of Rutgers University, 30 students
bared their breasts during a student government confab in order
to protest what they called the college's "patronizing attitude
toward women."  The whole exercise was a bit too much for
Washington Post scribe Tony Kornheiser, who said that while he
covers a variety of stories in the name of the First Amendment,
this one nearly threw him for a loop.
     According to Kornheiser, the AP story on this oh-so-serious
subject said that "The college is imposing stereotypes, myths and
structures upon women that define us.  We want to be able to
carve our own paths, take our own directions, and not be punished
or chastised for it," said Jeane Lociero (34-22-34).
     (*The last item, Tony assures readers, is a joke).
     Clearly baffled, Tony says, "Let me try and understand this:
Women who are mad about stereotyping, who want to be treated as
women, not sex objects...have protested by taking off their
shirts.  Apparently, part of the protest was because the school
said it did not have enough money to purchase new safety lights."
     "For this, women took off their clothes?" asks Tony.  "What
ever happened to bake sales?"

***

     Primal Scream,101: If you're "mad as hell and not going to
take it anymore," one way to let off steam would be to attend a
conference called "Rage! Across the Disciplines" that will be
held next month at Cal State U., San Marcos, according to the
Chronicle of Higher Education.
     The two-day event will feature presentations by scholars,
activists and artists dealing with "AIDS, gay rage, women's rage,
the rage of ethnic minorities and working class rage."  Librarian
and conference co-organizer Terry Allison explains that a major
goal of the gathering is to get attendees "to feel stirred by the
anger of the various panelists."

***

     Dumbing Down: A recent survey conducted by Penn State
students revealed more dismal stats, i.e. that 11 percent of Ivy
Leaguers had no idea that Thomas Jefferson wrote the Declaration
of Independence while 33 percent couldn't identify Lech Walesa as
leader of the Polish union "Solidarity."
     Among the other survey results: 34 percent didn't know that
John Major is the prime minister of Britain, 44 percent didn't
know that Tom Foley is speaker of the House, and a hefty 60
percent couldn't even name the two U.S. Senators from their home
state.
     Prof. Frank Luntz, a former pollster for Ross Perot and Pat
Buchanan, conducted the poll as a study project. It surveyed
3,119 students at Harvard, Yale, Princeton, Brown, Cornell
Columbia, Dartmouth and Penn.  Penn student and history major Ed
Miller said the survey results left him feeling a bit
downhearted.  "If Ivy Leaguers are the nation's leaders of
tomorrow, it doesn't bode well for the future," he said.

***

     High On The Hog: Newbury College president Edward Tassinari
certainly has very little to complain about regarding his living
conditions.  The school, located in Brookline, Mass., provides
Tassinari with a $900,000 home, a $229,700 salary and an
additional $43,712 in benefits and compensation, according to a
recent piece in the Boston Globe.
     The Globe also reported that Tassinari apparently gets
reimbursed by his employer for items like his liquid diet plan
and his son's wedding rehearsal dinner.  Then too, his son and
daughter-in-law were reportedly allowed to live in a
college-owned brownstone in exchange for shoveling snow and
helping with some renovations.
     What's the reaction of Newbury's board of trustees about
these financial arrangements?  So far, not much.  The trustees,
you see, are among those doing the most business with the
two-year college.  According to the Globe, trustee Frederick
Chicos' insurance company handles the schol's insurance coverage.
Trustee David Weltman, a partner in the law firm of Foley, Hoag
and Elliot, has glombed up a hefty $350,000 in business from
Newbury since 1988.  During the same period, the school paid
another $350,000 to trustee David Grossman and two firms where he
worked.  And the list goes on.

***

     Better "Red" Than Dead? Dr. Paul Risser, new president of
Miami University of Ohio, has called for a series of meetings to
discuss the latest bone of contention, namely the school's
nickname, "Miami Redskins."
     In a recent Cincinnati Enquirer editorial, the conflict was
viewed simply as a natural outgrowth of the PC movement that
deals more with political clout than real sensitivity.  The
editorial says that obviously no one picks a college nickname
with the intention of offending anyone but rather selects the
names "because they embody qualities and characteristics to be
admired and emulated."  The Indian image, they point out, is
light years away from the caricature/cartoon rendering used by
Notre Dame, Nevada-Las Vegas and other major universities.
     Indeed, Miami U. outlaws any derogatory use of Indian
symbols. And if that weren't enough, the editorial says that some
of the Miami Indians' top-notch tribal leaders approved the name
in 1972 and again in 1991.  Is this another case of the "problem"
residing not with supposed target of criticism but with the whiny
libs who purport to be allies of the "oppressed" and
"downtrodden?"

***

     Our Bodies, Our Lives: Students in the physician associates
program at Yale University School of Medicine are trained to give
the kind of hands-on clinical care that nurses and doctors are
often too busy to five.  Associate Dean Robert Gifford explains
that during the first year, students practice the basic elements
of the physical exam, including breast exams, on each other.
Then at the end of the first year, according to a recent piece in
Lingua Franca, they "attempt their first rectal, pelvic and
genital exams on "standardized patents," who are paid subjects
that allow their private parts to be probed in the public
interest.
     Last fall, when student Nadine Nicotera objected to
undergoing a breast exam by members of her class, her instructor
informed her that she risked failing the course if she did not
comply.  In subsequent talks with the school's deans and protests
by medical students, however, it was pointed out that male PA
students did not have to undergo the course's testicular exam.
Suddenly Nicotera was granted an exemption.
     Why can MD stdents practice on strangers while PA students
must practice on their classmates?  PA associate dean Elaine
Grant maintained that the hysical component is taught as "part of
the flow," and that class members are paired up according to how
comfortable they feel with each other.  The brouhaha that erupted
after this incident, however, combined with the fact that 85
percent of American med schools are using trained exam subjects,
caused the school to re-examine its practices and rather quickly
to adopt the "standardized patient" policy at this school also.
Dean Robert Gifford summed it up, saying, "When I learned about
the breast business, I felt the student had the absolute right to
opt out."

