How Professors Play the Cat Guarding the Cream.  Richard M.
Huber. 201 pages

William F. Meehan III

     Remember Bologna! is an exclamation used by Richard Huber in
a chapter titled Students, a chapter which is mainly about the
misdirected and fledgling efforts of the students-as-consumers
movement.  After pointing out that the student/consumer idea
won't work, Huber explains that during the 13th and 14th
centuries, students at Bologna University in northern Italy
entered into what amounts to contracts with their professors.  In
short, a professor had to meet his obligations to his students in
order to be paid; if he didn't, he paid.  Also, as a way of
ensuring that a professor covered all parts of a book equally, he
was required not only to follow a syllabus, but also to deposit a
small sum in an escrow account, which was debited if he fell
behind the schedule.
     Huber's point in Remember Bologna! is not so much to urge
students in the last decade of this century to follow in the
footsteps of the medievalists but instead to ask why professors
ensconced in tenure during the late 1900s can't be held
accountable as teachers, as were the professors at universities
in Europe during the Middle Ages.
     In How Professors Play the Cat Guarding the Cream, Huber
argues that the faculty of American research universities have
caused tuition to skyrocket, undergraduate teaching to become a
forgotten art, scholarly productivity to drop off, and
professional prestige to replace personal honor.  He illustrates
how professors (the costliest item in the budget at most schools)
hold the key to making universities more productive and less
expensive by doing what they are hired to do:  teach
undergraduates.
     But, says Huber, faculty can't be counted on to do the right
thing because "people will demonstrate an irresistible proclivity
towards self-seeking rather than self-giving."  In Huber's study,
this means that professors will not teach but instead will
research and publish because that, not teaching, is honored with
tenure--a job for life, a salary for life.
     In making his case that professors can help make their
universities more productive and less expensive Huber, a former
professor and dean who now works for the Department of State,
gives readers a course in university culture that explores the
hallowed halls of academia.  As a result of his tactful treatment
of an emotional subject, Huber's right-of-center perspective does
not slip into dogmatic boasting; it criticizes one of the
country's most prestigious professions but does not mock it.
Huber is the loyal embassy attache who doesn't want to hurt too
many locals' feelings.
     The heart of the matter in How Professors Play the Cat
Guarding the Cream is the argument that professors at research
universities, or at colleges that want to be research
universities, brazenly seek to teach less in order to
research/publish more because promotion depends on researching/
publishing and not on teaching.  These professors who spend their
time in the library or in the lab pursuing this end, not in the
classroom teaching, still receive their salaries; teaching
assistants and adjunct faculty are then hired to teach courses
that otherwise should be taught by full-time professors already
on the payroll.  Furthermore, administrators endorse what is
called "release time" for professors so they can publish because
the more recognition bestowed on a publishing professor, the more
recognition bestowed on the university, which in turn attracts
more government, corporate or foundation funding, which then
allows more professors to research.
     The idea of a university and of a professor is clear to
Huber:  "The educational mission of the university is not
supposed to be about the self-interest of the teacher but the
self-development of the students.  Teachers do not pursue
ambitious personal goals but rather guide others to their goals."
When a university seeks to be a player in the field of scholarly
research, its mission shifts away from teaching.  The result is
"a reduction in productivity because faculty teaching loads are
driven down equally within departments while scholarly
contributions are unequal."
     Huber offers several "doables," reforms that can raise
productivity and lower costs.  these include peer review, teacher
training, "growth compacts," meaningful accounting, early
retirement, technology, and differential faculty workloads.  But
adopting the last option, professors who believe they are chosen
to be superstars will not have to suffer the humiliation of
forming young minds, while professors who believe they are called
to teach will not have to endure the harangue of publishing or
perishing.
     Ever the diplomat, Huber seeks a win-win situation for
everyone, from professors to taxpayers.  He assures the targets
of his criticism:  "The reforms do not threaten the job security
and income of tenured professors.  They do not drain energies
dedicated to increasing the prestige of the university through
published work."  At first glance, this appears to be a
Clintonesque proposal--a promise of all things to all groups, a
cover for business as usual.  But further analysis reveals that
Huber might be right.  Maybe the scholar/researcher and the
scholar/teacher can coexist as universities.  Maybe those who
harbor "pretensions to research excellence" can work with those
who practice "the art of teaching."  Maybe those who seek
prestige can walk the halls with those who deserve honor.
     Until that day, however, universities operations will be
business as usual.  Productivity will go down more and costs will
go up more.  Universities will not seek remedies because,
according to Huber, parents believe that the more the education
costs the better it is:  "The student/parent demand for
prestigious degrees exceeds the collegiate supply.  There are
more aspiring elitists than there are spaces in elite colleges."
The unrestrained pursuit of prestige by faculty, though, is a
"grasping but glorious hunger: says Huber.  When will it ever be
satisfied?  There's only one way:  the cat can't guard the cream.

