POSTPONEMENT OF POLITICAL CORRECTNESS PROSECUTIONS
MAY TERMINATE NOMINATION OF CLINTON HUMANITIES CHIEF

By Michael J. Bennett

     The latest postponement of an official prosecution of a
politically incorrect student at the University of Pennsylvania
could set the stage for a Senate debate on the issue--and
termination of the appointment of Clinton's humanities chief.
     The trial of Eden Jacobowitz, an Israeli born Jew accused of
racial harassment for calling boisterous black sorority women
"water buffalo," was "indefinitely postponed," by university
officials without warning on April 26.  The postponement was
believed by many on the Ivy League campus to have been ordered by
University President Sheldon Hackney to protect his nomination as
chairman of the National Endowment for the Humanities.
     "My initial take on this is it (the postponement) is
designed to let Hackney get through this crisis quietly," said
Damon Chetson, associate editor of The Daily Pennsylvanian, the
school's independent student newspaper.
     Unidentified school officials shared Chetson's beliefs,
according to The Washington Times of April 27.
     "This travesty should generate Senate debate of political
correctness in colleges and universities and terminate Hackney's
nomination," said Jamey Wheeler, executive director of Accuracy
in Academia. "The Senate will be derelict in its duty if it does
not look into the systematic violation of personal, academic and
press freedom on the University of Pennsylvania campus in the
name of political correctness," Wheeler said.
     The incident is the latest of several in which alleged
racial harassment charges have arisen out of disputes between
white students trying to study or sleep and blacks holding
parties.  A week before the charges against Jacobowitz--which
could be raised again next September--were dropped black students
stole and dumped into the trash 14,000 copies of The Daily
Pennsylvanian.
     The raid was prompted by the university's dropping similar
charges against a columnist for the paper who had come to the
defense of two freshmen expelled from their room.  Their offense
was dumping water on members of a black honor society who
conducted an initiation ceremony at 2:30 in the morning under
dormitory windows.  The university was forced to admit it had no
legal jurisdiction over the columnist for the independent
newspaper which is protected by the First Amendment.
     Jacobowitz faces possible expulsion for telling a group of
black women they were acting like "water buffalo," and should go
to a nearby "zoo."  They were partying under his window while he
was trying to write an English paper and his roommate was
sleeping.  Dan Ben-Amos, an expert on black folklore, was
prepared to testify that Jacobowitz--a native-born Israeli fluent
in Hebrew--chose the phrase, "water buffalo," as the English
equivalent of the common Hebrew phrase "bahemen," which means
water oxen.  Both Jocobowitz and Ben-Amos denied the phrase is a
racial slur.
     Racial harassment charges have also been lodged against a
campus police officer who arrested one of the black students who
stole the copies of The Daily Pennsylvanian.
     "This is much worse than McCarthyism was," said Alan Charles
Kors, a history professor who had assembled a team of faculty and
students to testify on behalf of Jacobowitz.  "You can't say
anything," added Kors, who personally intervened with Hackney to
get the university charges against the columnist, Gregory
Pavlick, dismissed.
     Hackney has a national reputation as a free speech advocate
for his defense of photographers Robert Mapplethorpe and Andres
Serrano.  Mapplethorpe is noted for his homoerotic photos,
including one of a man urinating in another man's mouth.
Serrano's "Piss Christ" shows a crucifix standing in a jar of the
artist's urine.
     "It's easy to defend Mapplethorpe and Serrano," Chetson
said, "because these are politically correct people to defend.
These are people liberals love to defend.  It's much more
difficult to defend conservatives.  Hackney's taken a lot of
flack over this and he wants it to go away."
     Hackney has "betrayed both free speech and diversity,"
Stephen Glass, executive editor of The Daily Pennsylvanian said
in a signed column, "Hackney's Betrayal," April 20.  "Once known
for his defense of a controversial Robert Mapplethorpe art
exhibit, Hackney, in his so-called effort to 'balance' the two
ideals has betrayed both."
     Black students at the University of Pennsylvania complained
of both "blatant and covert racism."  Charges against one of the
students stealing the paper were dismissed after the officer was
accused of striking the student twice with a mini-baton.  The
officer has been assigned to desk duty while brutality charges
against him are considered.
     "They (the charges) have to be believed," Adelaide Delluva,
the university safety and security council chairperson, was
quoted in The Daily Pennsylvanian April 20.  "In my opinion, the
depth of racism is pretty appalling.  I almost blush with shame
when I hear about these incidents."
     "I don't want our police to turn out like the police in Los
Angeles," Delluva said, noting that University Police
Commissioner John Kuprevich, "has a very tough burden to
overcome.  He can take the police and try to discipline them, but
it won't change the basic attitude, which is a pretty rotten
one."  The Daily Pennsylvanian quoted her as adding "she is
concerned Kuprevich has a tough job ahead of him, adding, "I
don't know if he can make it."
     The dumping is believed to have been prompted by columns
written by Pavlick, a conservative columnist who has been
critical of Martin Luther King and Malcolm X.  A Pavlick column
in March criticized university officials for punishing two white
freshmen who dumped water on members of the Onyx Senior Honor
Society who were holding an initiation/hazing ceremony under
their dormitory windows at 2:30 a.m.  The column ignited a
firestorm of pro-and-con comment in the letters column.  The
water dumping students were expelled from their room, but Pavlick
argued it would have been more fair if the Onyx society was
"kicked off campus and it would have been if its members were
white."
     The university's Judicial Action Office reacted to the
Pavlick column by bringing 32 charges of "racial harassment"
against him, despite the fact that the paper is completely
independent, financially and legally.  Catherine C. Shifter, the
Judicial Action Officer, said she filed the complaint because she
was "afraid for his (Pavlick's) safety.  A lot of people were
upset with him as you can see from the letters to the editor.
And his picture is on his column."
     The judicial process is conducted behind closed doors unless
the defendant demands otherwise, Shifter said.  The charges were
dropped only after Hackney personally intervened at the request
of Kors, a professor of intellectual history and critic of
political correctness who was been quoted extensively by Arthur
Schlesinger in his book, The Disuniting of America: Reflections
on a Multicultural Society.
     "This is a classic example of political correctness or, more
accurately, political cowardice, destroying both academic and
press freedom on campus," said Jamey Wheeler, executive director
of Accuracy in Acdemia.  "This incident cannot be ignored by the
members of the Senate Subcommittee on Education, Arts and the
Humanities in the confirmation process.  To do so would be an
insult to students, faculty members and parents everywhere who
cannot understand why university officials are throwing up
artificial barriers among ethnic groups on the campus.  These
barriers to open discussion and debate, unhampered by adult
interference, cannot be allowed.  We don't need apartheid on
American college campuses."
     Hillary Clinton is scheduled to give the keynote address at
the university's commencement ceremonies May 17.  She and
Hackney's wife, Lucy, served together on the board of the
Childrens' Defense Fund.  Hackney's nomination as chairman of the
NEH has been pending since December, before Clinton's
inauguration, the student paper reported April 19.

