stan.koper@grapevine.lrk.ar.us (Stan Koper)  writes:

>............
>... PUG Said:... 6/05/1993..... Boston, MA . 06/07/1993......................
>. ............                                                              .
>.Heck, with a lot of users, it is probably just jwsmith or some such        .
>.thing. You can always just throw a dictionary at the encrypted passwd      .
>.and get a match. A shadow passwd file makes the passwd file useless        .
>.since you have to actually try to login in order to see if the passwd      .
>.works. If you have the encrypted passwd, such as at a lot of sites, then   .
>.you can simply encrypt what you think is they passwd and do a comparison   .
>.with what is actually the passwd. If there is a match, you have the        .
>.passwd without setting off any alarms from trying to login without the     .
>.passwd.                                                                    .
>.............................................................................
>Wait wait wait.  Now you're losing me (again?).  Let me see:

>1.  "just throw a dictionary at the encrypted password and get a
>match."
>Nice imagery.  Do you mean to say that most people select
>passwords from words which are ordinarily found in a dictionary,
>so all you need to do is encrypt a dictionary (using the same
>encryption method? how is that done?) and then comparing
>"words"  So that if you see "triuntl" in the passwd file,
>you look through your encrypted dictionary, and find that
>"triuntl" in there means "mommies", so you have a *possible*
>password?

Nothing so sophisicated. What you do is try all 30,000, words,
one at a time. This is the sort of thing that computers do well
at. You can either try to sign on with all of the passwords, or
you can use your own (or someone else's hopefully) userid, and
change the current password to each of the words in the dictionary,
then look to see if its encryption matches in the password file
(assuming no shadow password file - see below).

Apparently, most passwords are either
1) a variation on the userid,
2) a real word, or
3) something equally obvious (such as "asdfg" or "qwerty")
Why? Because you have to remember it. Have you ever tried to
remember a computer generated password? Much more difficult.

>2.  "A shadow passwd file makes the password file useless since
>you actually have to log in to see if the password works."
>Am I to understand then that a "shadow passwd" file does *not*
>contain the actual password, but something like a pointer for
>each user that points to another file which *does* contain the
>password?  JWSmith is user #3907, #3907 points to password
>file #3907 (or hey, any other number, right) which contains
>"triuntl", and which just coincidentally decrypts as "mommies"?

This is a popular UNIX security enhancement.
The publically available password file has a pointer to the
actual password. The actual password, instead of being in
encrypted form in the publically visible fle, is in a second,
highly protected (root access only) file.

>Have I got it so far?

>So the file printed out in this case was a "shadow passwd" file, which
>means it did *not* contain the actual passwords.  The crime was in printing
>out a list (apparently also available online) of names of users, along with
>selected identifying information such as might be found in a
>student/staff directory, from which individuals' passwords "might" be
>derived, *if* you relied on the normal stupidity and/or laziness of
>users in selecting passwords.  Moreover, the same information could be
>obtained from other published sources?

>Have I got it right?

Yes.

>So this guy was suspended for printing what amounted to a copy of a
>user phone book, with *no* passwords in it whatsoever?  But because
>the file was labelled "passwd", everyone acted as though it actually
>contained real, true passwords?  And that "everyone" included the
>*sysadmin*, who either did not know better, or if he did, did not
>attempt to disabuse anyone else of the misconception that there were
>passwords in the passwd file?

>Have I got it?

I would guess that what happened is that the administration reacted
when they heard that he had accessed the "password file". By the
time that they found out there hadn't been a security breach, it
was too late - they were committed. Better to get him on trumped up
bogus charges than to admit that they had gone off half-cocked.

